[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-102945"] :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $ϕ=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nϕ=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the angle that the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis must equal $\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, since $\\rho \\ge 0,$ our surface is a half-cone.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/assets/27f9b580436d8e1aebeb4f150643a22266e9ad7f488b98e63f8a49dd4f1aa97f.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-135302/selection/1435aae3eb77dceb26d97c218bdbfd31db48a3ff0355a0b689a611359a5b1a28" :answer/id #uuid "bfd184af-c443-5ce5-a494-704438845a83" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A paraboloid" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135302/selection/708f9bb98bee93bbddef60cfb15ebf104ab85a40d5d63de8246ae01fe212f809" :answer/id #uuid "5d2485ae-0fe9-5720-8d7d-f80a1a44c22c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A half-plane that passes through the $x$-axis" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135302/selection/629edb33ca1a99b9b321b4aa6f9cd868354c57a10386e13007d4b875cd623645" :answer/id #uuid "eca197b7-b7b0-58b5-bd1e-a520132f4d42" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A half-line" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135302/selection/e3bc01c1a7cc4eaaa3d0fec3c0eb87ff9cb3bf03f2891a742d43b82797869ffc" :answer/id #uuid "21ee4685-5180-5684-b220-d02334d56980" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A circular cylinder" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135302/selection/9a01d3b7bb50f74f0f0a937ec21e923b3fd4baedfed010beb5720f21b3f20cd5" :answer/id #uuid "9554a28e-acbf-52a5-8994-f2259ae5c2f2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-135302/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ee3233f5-30ea-5c0d-9fc0-bf2017770155" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-135302/selection/9a01d3b7bb50f74f0f0a937ec21e923b3fd4baedfed010beb5720f21b3f20cd5" :answer-field/choices ["q-135302/selection/1435aae3eb77dceb26d97c218bdbfd31db48a3ff0355a0b689a611359a5b1a28" "q-135302/selection/708f9bb98bee93bbddef60cfb15ebf104ab85a40d5d63de8246ae01fe212f809" "q-135302/selection/629edb33ca1a99b9b321b4aa6f9cd868354c57a10386e13007d4b875cd623645" "q-135302/selection/e3bc01c1a7cc4eaaa3d0fec3c0eb87ff9cb3bf03f2891a742d43b82797869ffc" "q-135302/selection/9a01d3b7bb50f74f0f0a937ec21e923b3fd4baedfed010beb5720f21b3f20cd5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-135302" :question/id #uuid "91c21edd-cba7-59db-be76-718b72384d4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-135302" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the angle that the corresponding vector makes with the positive $x$-axis must equal $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$ On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, since $\\rho \\ge 0,$ our surface is a half-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/assets/0043ffdc885e7edeebb7c4df571466fe4f13dfb08728290cd6d54f47777a7179.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-135302/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a52e1346-75c4-537c-add9-777911dbe5ff"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-135302"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161803/selection/646bba0ca1abbde19da498556ace3eb4a9ebb7b119b724565de220107fb17e24" :answer/id #uuid "9841a52b-29ab-50fd-82c1-42d60649c513" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161803/selection/cfbf4007e89db8dfc02c6fa3a98e60cdf8223caa68f329ce3fc3184f4e620c44" :answer/id #uuid "bc18e2b0-42b8-594e-9c3d-2be82a719342" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le 2\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161803/selection/b8c5220f1290d205a87a12edd54e79459024d884a3bbcd575f19e368a73365f3" :answer/id #uuid "a5864725-e578-53a6-9cc9-b1442aee87d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161803/selection/3544511b1a67512ea047b867d8a56720eefc5a6d9798c4af95155ea1afc80710" :answer/id #uuid "53dab1c5-4f5e-5f48-a010-deadb89f38f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161803/selection/bd5b6b02b22b3ea7346fb84c6461043d979961d62a1d3f14ecca3c6947694fec" :answer/id #uuid "8cc51784-95a4-5419-9cc3-e21f84e37fc1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161803/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0a4917d1-b91b-5846-81aa-8c90d0c79594" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161803/selection/646bba0ca1abbde19da498556ace3eb4a9ebb7b119b724565de220107fb17e24" :answer-field/choices ["q-161803/selection/646bba0ca1abbde19da498556ace3eb4a9ebb7b119b724565de220107fb17e24" "q-161803/selection/cfbf4007e89db8dfc02c6fa3a98e60cdf8223caa68f329ce3fc3184f4e620c44" "q-161803/selection/b8c5220f1290d205a87a12edd54e79459024d884a3bbcd575f19e368a73365f3" "q-161803/selection/3544511b1a67512ea047b867d8a56720eefc5a6d9798c4af95155ea1afc80710" "q-161803/selection/bd5b6b02b22b3ea7346fb84c6461043d979961d62a1d3f14ecca3c6947694fec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161803" :question/id #uuid "9eae7513-d500-5ef8-82e2-f071bccc92fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-161803" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/assets/f8a15e2452febed245c0e0bc8a0e7cad8a3579f7205ec4a295fc36875b0d7141.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the hemisphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=16$ for $z\\ge 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/assets/85ebe508035ae538d6becd73e9563be903a8cfcc4764550e131c35c84f7b80de.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:  \n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,4]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi /2]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-161803/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fde60ded-ec05-5a2c-8844-26e8e02c9afb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161803"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161822/selection/a82ce6bb55e2dcb633a56b9f4495f631f3d642203d2cb512afa072c18b891482" :answer/id #uuid "2e5fcc02-e966-52d7-86b0-24cc45be5775" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6,\\,\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161822/selection/65c375836eb7c4b76e5b7c43598a0513e544348b5d05856294d3f02dffc86b95" :answer/id #uuid "c4ff2aca-6caa-5e35-8db5-5cd7e33fd721" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 3,\\,\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le 2\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161822/selection/096c1cf82dd168ea19755e65ba980d1e282660801d02e0228730d45e9a393a67" :answer/id #uuid "92fbce80-ebd0-5021-a715-0dc15267520b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4,\\,\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161822/selection/2af6307586cd3ad2af285ba5d34890451f2a513073d0e10fa306dc2893862c0f" :answer/id #uuid "70d86b5b-8e08-587b-8455-cc5d5b615338" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6,\\,\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161822/selection/03b72cb423e2266d4918a6c383edc6eb9a38b86e5349420c0ecd6d458b0ef40f" :answer/id #uuid "f845b37e-a469-5a63-b8cd-66cdfc73e74d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 9,\\,\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161822/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "86ea7f3d-e895-5646-b10c-adb24a46e87c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161822/selection/a82ce6bb55e2dcb633a56b9f4495f631f3d642203d2cb512afa072c18b891482" :answer-field/choices ["q-161822/selection/a82ce6bb55e2dcb633a56b9f4495f631f3d642203d2cb512afa072c18b891482" "q-161822/selection/65c375836eb7c4b76e5b7c43598a0513e544348b5d05856294d3f02dffc86b95" "q-161822/selection/096c1cf82dd168ea19755e65ba980d1e282660801d02e0228730d45e9a393a67" "q-161822/selection/2af6307586cd3ad2af285ba5d34890451f2a513073d0e10fa306dc2893862c0f" "q-161822/selection/03b72cb423e2266d4918a6c383edc6eb9a38b86e5349420c0ecd6d458b0ef40f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161822" :question/id #uuid "d33696cd-4eae-5405-88b6-fb8354c01742" :question/math-academy-id "q-161822" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/assets/6b22ae2aaa00d077f8204fcfde073b22241debdce7ea7f7bb7bbfaf0ec213a5d.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the quarter-sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=36$ for $y\\ge 0$ and $x\\le 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/assets/22c1271f2facac9a05548d4a94b5c41ecd68699f50af16f200687a2a3f94f1da.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary withing the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,6]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-161822/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fde60ded-ec05-5a2c-8844-26e8e02c9afb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161822"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161883/selection/5fe68a383fed5a93c7495db1f7c412021ac6fca07f15117e3f54a8000575eaec" :answer/id #uuid "4df0ce0a-2784-5ed1-98d7-dabe6b13640d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161883/selection/4a85b757302eddb7fa735e1944c3019bf5f52215418f7290f7a5279502d387bb" :answer/id #uuid "96de6de2-0978-5064-a1f9-d9c36d4487e9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161883/selection/95da3c429b4b28cd1a966bbb160df3555f28befc51105dbd4573e5d887bfef2a" :answer/id #uuid "480573a8-7d77-56e5-99eb-ce7dc6175b22" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161883/selection/7093909162b07d07e213867debdcebca12458bc30f05a42bc401f2c3474b8543" :answer/id #uuid "21d54970-20a9-5389-8e28-72b2265f3566" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161883/selection/beb923f23d6025f2016c2d56cdd163efdd18a32e7e8b8630097056ebf6d9b60d" :answer/id #uuid "ce45b738-890b-5edd-a611-a68d82977b06" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }\n$$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161883/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "860a109d-2fba-5b4c-997e-048d856105e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161883/selection/5fe68a383fed5a93c7495db1f7c412021ac6fca07f15117e3f54a8000575eaec" :answer-field/choices ["q-161883/selection/5fe68a383fed5a93c7495db1f7c412021ac6fca07f15117e3f54a8000575eaec" "q-161883/selection/4a85b757302eddb7fa735e1944c3019bf5f52215418f7290f7a5279502d387bb" "q-161883/selection/95da3c429b4b28cd1a966bbb160df3555f28befc51105dbd4573e5d887bfef2a" "q-161883/selection/7093909162b07d07e213867debdcebca12458bc30f05a42bc401f2c3474b8543" "q-161883/selection/beb923f23d6025f2016c2d56cdd163efdd18a32e7e8b8630097056ebf6d9b60d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161883" :question/id #uuid "932b92db-8d77-5894-b632-3b8edb79745e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161883" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/assets/a32e8fd24c990a831cfee9525c6b1c035fb4ee6e09deedb3ac4c899d90ceeaac.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the half-sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4y=0$ for $z\\le 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-half of the sphere of radius $2$ centered at $(0,-2,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4y=0$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4y & =0 \\\\ {\\rho }^{2}+4\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & =0 \\\\ \\rho (\\rho +4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069421/assets/e2865e838521a8864ad76d72e520d4b128a8fceb8c18cb9c35ba10134419c4eb.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-161883/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49ec24aa-1f87-5ce1-8ae3-ae4cb5462ae6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161883"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
